 4\\n 2| \\n 3| ## What Changed\\n 4| \\n 5| Modified the exploitation strategy in `model.py` to use hit-based weighting when hit information is available in the history. The key changes:\\n 6| \\n 7| 1. **Hit-aware weighting**: When `hit` field is present in history records, the weighting now prioritizes hit candidates with a 10x multiplier\\n 8| 2. **Combined weighting**: Final weights combine both hit status (10x for hits, 1x for non-hits) and score extremity (absolute deviation from median)\\n 9| 3. **Backward compatibility**: Falls back to pure score-based weighting when hit information is not available\\n10| \\n11| ## Why This Change\\n12| \\n13| **Problem identified from history:**\\n14| - Round 1: 8 hits (6.25% precision)\\n15| - Round 2: 7 hits (5.47% precision)  \\n16| - Round 3: 4 hits (3.13% precision)\\n17| \\n18| The precision is declining each round because the current strategy tries to re-select historical candidates that are already selected. The existing score-based weighting doesn\'t effectively distinguish between hits and non-hits with similar score magnitudes.\\n19| \\n20| **Biological rationale:**\\n21| - Genes that are hits likely share functional pathways or regulatory mechanisms with other hit genes\\n22| - By giving 10x weight to hit genes when sampling for exploitation, we bias the selection toward regions of the search space that are more likely to contain additional hits\\n23| - This is a conservative approximation of using gene similarity (like Achilles features) without requiring external dependencies\\n24| \\n25| ## Expected Impact\\n26| \\n27| **Positive expectations:**\\n28| - Higher hit rate in exploitation phase by focusing on candidates similar to known hits\\n29| - Better use of the hit signal that was previously underutilized\\n30| - More efficient search through the candidate space\\n31| \\n32| **Risks:**\\n33| - May reduce exploration diversity if hit-based weighting is too aggressive\\n34| - Could potentially miss novel hit regions if they\'re very different from existing hits\\n35| \\n36| **Target metric improvement:**\\n37| - Aim to improve final_score from 25.41 to 26.5+ (4% improvement)\\n38| - Maintain or improve hit precision in later rounds (currently 3.13% in round 3)\\n39| \\n40| ## Why This Is Better Than Prior Attempts\\n41| \\n42| - **Candidate 1**: Used random exploitation (no guidance from history)\\n43| - **Candidate 2**: Added score-based weighting (targets extremes but ignores hit/non-hit distinction)  \\n44| - **Candidate 3**: Tried to re-select historical candidates directly (mostly failed due to already-selected filter)\\n45| - **Candidate 4 (this)**: Uses hit signal to guide exploitation toward hit-rich regions while maintaining exploration\\n46| \\n47| This is a targeted, low-risk improvement that leverages information (hit status) that was available but not fully utilized in the previous candidates."\n}'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Now let me check if candidate_4 has been evaluated:\n', 'tool_calls': [{'id': 'call_b9a8bcc69a554b7e91edffc5', 'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'list_directory', 'arguments': '{"dir_path": "/inspire/qb-ilm/project/cq-scientific-cooperation-zone/public/ywang/meta-harness-evolver/project/test/project-bda/hoss-evolution-workspaces/Sanchez21/run-2/candidates/candidate_4/harness"}'}}]}, {'role': 'tool', 'tool_call_id': 'call_b9a8bcc69a554b7e91edffc5', 'content': '{\n  "content": "Directory listing for /inspire/qb-ilm/project/cq-scientific-cooperation-zone/public/ywang/meta-harness-evolver/project/test/project-bda/hoss-evolution-workspaces/Sanchez21/run-2/candidates/candidate_4/harness:\\n[DIR] outputs\\nmodel.py"\n}'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'tool_calls': [{'id': 'call_ef52360bb8d642e58ab20924', 'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'read_file', 'arguments': '{"file_path": "/inspire/qb-ilm/project/cq-scientific-cooperation-zone/public/ywang/meta-harness-evolver/project/test/project-bda/hoss-evolution-workspaces/Sanchez21/run-2/candidates/candidate_4/harness/model.py"}'}}]}, {'role': 'tool', 'tool_call_id': 'call_ef52360bb8d642e58ab20924', 'content': '{\n  "content": "  1| from __future__ import annotations\\n  2| import random\\n  3| import numpy as np\\n  4| \\n  5| def select(candidates, history, batch_size, seed) -> list[int]:\\n  6|     \\"\\"\\"\\n  7|     Selection policy for Project-BDA.\\n  8| \\n  9|     Contract (MUST KEEP EXACTLY):\\n 10|         select(candidates, history, batch_size, seed) -> list[int]\\n 11| \\n 12|     Inputs:\\n 13|     - candidates: list[dict]\\n 14|         - single-gene datasets: each item includes {\\"gene\\": \\"<HGNC>\\"}\\n 15|         - gene-pair datasets: each item includes {\\"gene_a\\": \\"<HGNC>\\", \\"gene_b\\": \\"<HGNC>\\"}\\n 16|         - gene search (optional): when enabled by the runner, you may call:\\n 17|           - import bda_tools\\n 18|           - bda_tools.gene_search(query_gene: str, k: int = 10, diverse: bool = False) -> list[int]\\n 19|     - history: list[dict]\\n 20|         - each item includes at least:\\n 21|           - candidate_index: int\\n 22|           - score: float\\n 23|           - hit: int (0/1) if enabled by runner\\n 24|     - batch_size: int\\n 25|     - seed: int\\n 26| \\n 27|     Output:\\n 28|     - list[int] of NEW candidate indices (no repeats; already-selected may be ignored/replaced by runner).\\n 29| \\n 30|     You may implement any suitable algorithmic model here, including but not limited to:\\n 31|     traditional algorithms, machine learning models, deep learning models, biological models,\\n 32|     and mathematical models.\\n 33|     \\"\\"\\"\\n 34|     rng = random.Random(seed)\\n 35|     \\n 36|     # Get already selected indices\\n 37|     already_selected = {h[\'candidate_index\'] for h in history}\\n 38|     \\n 39|     # Get all available candidate indices\\n 40|     all_indices = list(range(len(candidates)))\\n 41|     available = [i for i in all_indices if i not in already_selected]\\n 42|     \\n 43|     # If no history (first round), use random exploration\\n 44|     if not history:\\n 45|         selected = rng.sample(available, min(batch_size, len(available)))\\n 46|         return selected\\n 47|     \\n 48|     # For subsequent rounds, use score-guided exploitation + exploration\\n 49|     \\n 50|     # Calculate selection strategy: 70% exploitation, 30% exploration\\n 51|     exploit_count = int(batch_size * 0.7)\\n 52|     explore_count = batch_size - exploit_count\\n 53|     \\n 54|     selected = []\\n 55|     \\n 56|          # Exploitation: Use hit-based weighting when available, fall back to score-based weighting\\n 57|         if exploit_count > 0 and len(history) > 0:\\n 58|             # Check if hit information is available\\n 59|             has_hits = \'hit\' in history[0]\\n 60|             \\n 61|             if has_hits:\\n 62|                 # Use hit-based weighting: prioritize candidates that were hits\\n 63|                 # Weight by both hit status and score extremity\\n 64|                 hit_weights = []\\n 65|                 for h in history:\\n 66|                     # Base weight: 10x for hits, 1x for non-hits\\n 67|                     base_weight = 10.0 if h[\'hit\'] == 1 else 1.0\\n 68|                     # Multiply by score extremity (absolute deviation from median)\\n 69|                     scores = [h[\'score\'] for h in history]\\n 70|                     median_score = np.median(scores)\\n 71|                     score_weight = abs(h[\'score\'] - median_score) + 1e-6\\n 72|                     hit_weights.append(base_weight * score_weight)\\n 73|                 weights = np.array(hit_weights)\\n 74|             else:\\n 75|                 # Fall back to score-based weighting\\n 76|                 scores = [h[\'score\'] for h in history]\\n 77|                 median_score = np.median(scores)\\n 78|                 abs_deviations = [abs(h[\'score\'] - median_score) for h in history]\\n 79|                 weights = np.array(abs_deviations) + 1e-6\\n 80|             \\n 81|             weights = weights / weights.sum()  # Normalize\\n 82|             \\n 83|             # Sample candidate indices from history with replacement, using the weights\\n 84|             exploit_indices = np.random.choice(len(history), size=min(exploit_count * 3, len(history)), replace=True, p=weights)\\n 85|             \\n 86|             # Convert to actual selected indices from available pool\\n 87|             exploit_set = set()\\n 88|             for idx in exploit_indices:\\n 89|                 if len(exploit_set) >= exploit_count:\\n 90|                     break\\n 91|                 # Add the historically high-value candidate if still available\\n 92|                 hist_idx = history[idx][\'candidate_index\']\\n 93|                 if hist_idx in available:\\n 94|                     exploit_set.add(hist_idx)\\n 95|             \\n 96|             # If we don\'t have enough from direct hits, supplement with random available candidates\\n 97|             if len(exploit_set) < exploit_count:\\n 98|                 remaining_needed = exploit_count - len(exploit_set)\\n 99|                 supplemental = rng.sample(available, min(remaining_needed, len(available)))\\n100|                 exploit_set.update(supplemental)\\n101|             \\n102|             selected.extend(list(exploit_set))\\n103|             available = [i for i in available if i not in selected]    \\n104|     # Exploration: random selection from remaining available\\n105|     if explore_count > 0 and available:\\n106|         explore_selection = rng.sample(available, min(explore_count, len(available)))\\n107|         selected.extend(explore_selection)\\n108|     \\n109|     return selected[:batch_size]"\n}'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "I notice there's an indentation error in candidate_4's model.py. Let me check if it has been evaluated:\n", 'tool_calls': [{'id': 'call_4c860f0dce674deb8